\description{
Answers two questions about a career in the public service: how many more
years does an employee stay, and how does their real pay grow, from
hiring onward? It builds a whole career out of a panel that covers only a
few years, by joining up people seen at different stages of theirs.

Like \code{\link[=compute_service_table]{compute_service_table()}}, it follows an imaginary group of \code{radix}
new hires through the years, using the rates from
\code{\link[=estimate_progression_rates]{estimate_progression_rates()}}:
\enumerate{
\item \strong{Service.} Each year, a share \code{px} of the group stays. That gives
how many are still in service at each tenure (\code{lx}) and the expected
remaining years of service (\code{ex}).
\item \strong{Pay.} Starting from \code{entry_pay}, each year's \code{pay_growth} is
compounded into \code{projected_pay}: the pay of someone who stays, at each
year of service. \code{pay_index} is the same path with entry pay set to
100.
\item \strong{Earnings.} Years in service times pay, added up over the rest of the
career: \code{expected_earnings}.
}

\strong{The result is an imaginary career, not a forecast.} See Details.
}
\details{
\strong{Imaginary career, not a forecast.} The table assumes a new hire meets,
at every future year of service, the stay rates and pay growth seen at
that tenure in your panel. It describes the careers the panel's years
imply, not what will happen to anyone. A pay reform or hiring freeze in
those years shapes the whole table.

\strong{Why chain pay growth instead of averaging pay by tenure.} \code{mean_pay}
compares different people at each tenure: those at tenure 25 were hired
25 years ago, under other pay scales, and are the ones who stayed. The
chained \code{projected_pay} follows the pay changes of the same people from
one year to the next, so it is not distorted by who was hired when or who
left. The gap between the two shows how much that matters.

\strong{Where a career starts.} Each group's career starts at the first tenure
it was seen at (\code{start_tenure}). If no one in a group was seen at tenure
0, for example an occupation people are promoted into rather than hired
into, its career starts later, with a warning naming the group. With
\code{entry_pay = NULL} the starting pay is the median at that tenure.

\strong{Gaps.} Both chains need a stay rate and a pay growth for every year
from a group's first tenure to its last. A year with no one in it, or no
one who stayed on pay, is a gap, and the rates are smoothed to fill it,
with a warning. Missing pay growth in a group's last year is not a gap,
since it is never used.

\strong{The table ends at the highest tenure in the data.} \code{ex} and
\code{expected_earnings} count nothing beyond it.

\strong{Units of \code{expected_earnings}.} It is in the units of \code{salary_col} per
year of service. If pay is monthly, multiply by 12 for yearly earnings.
}
